  "ch2.cv.2022": "2022",
  "ch2.say.up": "Yield up {dy} pp: the 2-year falls {p2}%, while the 30-year falls <em>{p30}%</em>—{ratio} times as much. For the {T}-year, duration alone predicts {dur}%, duration plus convexity gives {dc}%, and the exact move is {ex}%.",
  "ch2.say.down": "Yield down {dy} pp: the 2-year rises {p2}%, and the 30-year rises <em>{p30}%</em>. For the {T}-year, duration alone predicts +{dur}%, less than the exact +{ex}%: convexity softens drops and amplifies rallies.",
  "ch2.say.zero": "Yield unchanged, price unchanged. Drag Δy.",
  "ch2.say.y2022": "In 2022, the 2-year yield rose from 0.73% to 4.41%, the 10-year from 1.52% to 3.88%, and the 30-year from 1.90% to 3.97%. Repricing hypothetical par bonds issued at end-2021 yields to end-2022 yields cuts their prices by {p2}%, {p10}% and <em>{p30}%</em> (ignoring coupons received during the year and the one-year shortening of maturity).",
  "ch2.p3": "That is exactly what happened in 2022. Starting on March 17, 2022, the Federal Reserve raised rates and yields jumped across every maturity. According to the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), US banks ended 2022 sitting on roughly $620 billion in unrealised securities losses. Silicon Valley Bank had put a huge share of its deposits into long-dated securities: the Fed's April 2023 post-mortem reported that at end-2022 its held-to-maturity portfolio had a weighted-average duration of 6.2 years, and about 94% of its deposits exceeded the insurance limit. By the duration rule, each 1-percentage-point rise in yields wiped roughly 6% off that portfolio's market value. Accounting rules kept those losses off the income statement, but once depositors ran and the bank was forced to sell, the losses became real. Regulators closed it on March 10, 2023. For a model of how a bank with no bad loans can still collapse, see <a href=\"../where-money-comes-from/#ch3\">Chapter 3 of Where Money Comes From</a>.",
  "ch2.p4": "Duration is not unique to bonds. Every 'stream of future money' has a duration and the same sensitivity to rates: equities and mortgages (Chapter 8), and the government's interest bill (Chapter 11).",
  "ch2.see": "For an intuitive explanation of why higher rates force old bond prices down, see <a href=\"../../../investment/rates/interest-rates-bonds/#step4\">Step 4 of Interest Rates & Bonds</a>.",
  "ch2.limit": "Limits of the model: no default risk, annual coupons, and parallel yield shifts across all maturities. In practice, yields move by different amounts at different maturities (in 2022 the short end rose most)—which brings us to the next chapter.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "The Yield Curve: Embedded Prices for Every Future Year",
  "ch3.law": "Line up yields by maturity and peel the curve back year by year to read off the one-year rate for a future year that the market will lock in today—the forward rate.",
  "ch3.p1": "A <strong>yield curve</strong> plots yields across maturities for the same borrower (usually a government). Each day the US Treasury publishes 'par yields': the coupon rate that would make a hypothetical bond at each maturity trade at exactly 100.",
  "ch3.p2": "A par yield is an average. A 10-year bond's ten coupons arrive in ten different years, and each ought to be discounted at its own year's rate. Starting from year 1 and solving year by year extracts the discount factor for each maturity—a procedure called <strong>bootstrapping</strong>. From those discount factors we get <strong>spot rates</strong> (zero-coupon rates, the yield on a bond paying a single sum in year <i>n</i>) and <strong>forward rates</strong> (the one-year borrowing rate from year <i>n</i>−1 to year <i>n</i> that you can lock in today).",
  "ch3.eq": "1 = <i>c<sub>n</sub></i>·(<i>DF</i><sub>1</sub> + … + <i>DF</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>) + (1 + <i>c<sub>n</sub></i>)·<i>DF<sub>n</sub></i><br><i>z<sub>n</sub></i> = <i>DF<sub>n</sub></i><sup>−1/<i>n</i></sup> − 1&emsp;&emsp;<i>f</i><sub><i>n</i>−1→<i>n</i></sub> = <i>DF</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>/<i>DF<sub>n</sub></i> − 1",
  "ch3.model.title": "Bootstrapping spot and forward rates from US Treasury par yields",
  "ch3.model.how": "White dots are published US Treasury par yields for that date (1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 10, 20 and 30 years). Pick a date to compare the cyan spot curve and the gold forward curve, then drag 'Maturity <i>n</i>' to read that year's par yield, spot rate, one-year forward rate and discount factor.",
  "ch3.model.aria": "US Treasury par yields for a selected date alongside the bootstrapped spot curve and one-year forward curve",
