  "ch3.unit.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch3.unit.years": "{v} yr",
  "ch3.cv.x": "Maturity (years) →",
  "ch3.cv.y": "↑ Rate (%)",
  "ch3.say.one": "At year 1 there are no earlier years to strip out, so all three rates are equal: {z}%. Drag right.",
  "ch3.say.inverted": "On this date the curve is <em>inverted</em>: the {n}-year spot rate is {z}%, below the 1-year rate of {z1}%. The forward rate from Year {n1} to Year {n} is just <em>{f}%</em>—the future rate the market locks in today is lower than today's.",
  "ch3.say.normal": "On this date the curve slopes upward: the {n}-year spot rate is {z}%, above the 1-year rate of {z1}%. The forward rate from Year {n1} to Year {n} is <em>{f}%</em>.",
  "ch3.p3": "At the end of June 2023 the curve was inverted: 5.40% at 1 year, a 10-year spot rate of 3.76%, and a forward rate from Year 9 to Year 10 of just 3.24%. At the end of 2021 the opposite held: 0.39% at 1 year and a 1.78% forward rate from Year 9 to Year 10. Forward rates swing more than spot rates because a forward rate isolates the marginal year, whereas a spot rate averages all the years leading up to it. The next chapter asks: what determines those forward rates?",
  "ch3.limit": "Limits of the model: missing par maturities are linearly interpolated with annual coupons. The Treasury's own curve uses semi-annual coupons and a smoothing spline, so these spot rates differ from the official zero-coupon curve by a few basis points. Linear interpolation also creates steps in the forward curve at knot maturities (such as the jump around year 20)—that comes from the interpolation method, not the market.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "Why the Curve Takes Its Shape: Expectations Plus a Waiting Fee",
  "ch4.law": "A long-term rate is roughly the average of expected future short rates plus a term premium compensating for long-term risk. Curves usually invert because markets expect rate cuts ahead.",
  "ch4.p1": "You can buy a 10-year Treasury, or buy a 1-year bill and roll it over ten times. If investors cared only about average return, both strategies would offer the same expected payoff, making the 10-year rate the average of expected short rates over the next decade. That is the <strong>expectations hypothesis</strong>. Locking in for ten years, however, exposes you to interim rate swings and inflation surprises, so investors typically demand extra compensation—the <strong>term premium</strong>.",
  "ch4.eq": "<i>y<sub>n</sub></i> ≈ (1/<i>n</i>)·∫<sub>0</sub><sup><i>n</i></sup> E[<i>i<sub>t</sub></i>] d<i>t</i> + <i>TP<sub>n</sub></i>&emsp;&emsp;E[<i>i<sub>t</sub></i>] = <i>i<sub>L</sub></i> + (<i>i</i><sub>0</sub> − <i>i<sub>L</sub></i>)·2<sup>−<i>t</i>/<i>h</i></sup>",
  "ch4.p2": "Here we use the simplest expected path: the short rate moves from today's <i>i</i><sub>0</sub> toward a long-run level <i>i<sub>L</sub></i>, halving the gap every <i>h</i> years. The term premium rises linearly with maturity up to <i>TP</i><sub>10</sub> at year 10 and is flat thereafter. That shape is an assumption for illustration; real-world term premia have to be estimated with a model, as shown below.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Expectations + term premium = yield curve",
  "ch4.model.how": "The dashed grey line is your chosen 'expected short-rate path', the cyan line is the yield curve from expectations alone, and the gold line adds the term premium. With the defaults—4% today, 3% long-run—the market expects cuts. Pull the long-run rate lower to see the curve invert, then raise the term premium to see it un-invert.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Expected short-rate path, expectations-only yield curve, and yield curve including term premium",
  "ch4.leg.path": "Expected short rate",
  "ch4.leg.exp": "Expectations-only yield",
  "ch4.leg.yield": "Expectations + term premium",
  "ch4.ctl.i0": "Short rate today",
