  "ch4.ctl.h": "Half-life (years)",
  "ch4.ctl.tp": "10-year term premium",
  "ch4.read.y3m": "3-month yield",
  "ch4.read.y10": "10-year yield",
  "ch4.read.sp": "10-year minus 3-month",
  "ch4.read.tp": "Of which term premium",
  "ch4.unit.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch4.unit.pp": "{v} pp",
  "ch4.unit.years": "{v} yr",
  "ch4.cv.x": "Maturity / future year →",
  "ch4.cv.y": "↑ Rate (%)",
  "ch4.say.inverted": "The 10y–3m spread is <em>{sp}</em> pp, so the curve is inverted: the market expects the short rate to fall from {i0}% toward {iL}%, pulling long yields below short yields; the term premium is {tp} pp.",
  "ch4.say.invtp": "The spread is <em>{sp}</em> pp and the curve is inverted—not because cuts are expected, but because the term premium is negative ({tp} pp): investors are accepting lower yields to hold long bonds.",
  "ch4.say.flat": "The spread is only {sp} pp, leaving the curve nearly flat: in the 10-year yield, expectations contribute {e}% and the term premium adds {tp} pp, matching the 3-month rate.",
  "ch4.say.normal": "The spread is <em>{sp}</em> pp, sloping upward. In the 10-year yield, expectations contribute {e}% and the term premium adds {tp} pp.",
  "ch4.p3": "The term premium is unobservable and must be estimated from data. Two widely cited estimates are the Federal Reserve Board's Kim–Wright model and the New York Fed's ACM model, which sometimes differ by several tenths of a percentage point. The purple line below shows the Kim–Wright 10-year term premium (monthly average): peaking around 2.45 percentage points in the early 1990s, falling to about −0.61 around 2020, and sitting near 0.9 in early November 2026. Next, look at the yield curve's most famous application: inversions and recessions.",
  "ch4.hist.title": "The US 10-year minus 3-month spread and recessions",
  "ch4.hist.how": "The cyan line is the 10-year Treasury yield minus the 3-month T-bill rate (monthly average); red shading marks recessions dated by the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER). Drag the 'lead window': an inversion within that many months before a recession starts counts as 'called'; an inversion with no recession starting within that window counts as a 'false alarm'.",
  "ch4.hist.aria": "US 10-year minus 3-month spread, Kim–Wright term premium, and NBER recessions since 1960",
  "ch4.leg.spread": "10y − 3m spread",
  "ch4.leg.kw": "Term premium (Kim–Wright)",
  "ch4.leg.rec": "Recession (NBER)",
  "ch4.ctl.lead": "Lead window",
  "ch4.read.called": "Recessions preceded by inversion",
  "ch4.read.miss": "Missed",
  "ch4.read.false": "False alarms (no recession)",
  "ch4.read.now": "Latest spread ({m})",
  "ch4.unit.months": "{v} months",
  "ch4.unit.frac": "{a} / {b}",
  "ch4.unit.count": "{v}",
  "ch4.unit.none": "None",
  "ch4.unit.ym": "{y}-{m}",
  "ch4.unit.sep": ", ",
  "ch4.say.hist": "With a {lead}-month window: of the {n} recessions with a full data window, <em>{c}</em> were preceded by an inversion; missed: {miss}. False alarms: {fa} (inversions starting {falist}).",
  "ch4.p4": "With a 12-month window, 7 of the 8 recessions since 1960 with a full window were preceded by an inversion; the missed one was 1990 (in this monthly series, the spread neared zero in 1989 without turning negative). There were 4 false alarms. Widening the window to 24 months turns the late-1978 and 2006 inversions into hits, leaving 2 false alarms: 1966, and the inversion that began in November 2022 and lasted two years. As of October 2026, the NBER has declared no new recession since 2020. That is the actual record: a high hit rate, lead times anywhere from six months to a year and a half, only 8 events, and the latest one missed.<span class=\"tag m\">Contested</span>Why it works is debated: one view says inversion reflects expectations of future rate cuts because the economy is slowing; another says inversion itself squeezes banks' borrow-short, lend-long margins and tightens credit. Others note that low or negative term premia in recent years mean a given inversion implies less expected easing than in the past.",
  "ch4.limit": "Limits of the model: US recessions only—a small sample, so any claim that the indicator 'never fails' overstates the evidence. This series uses monthly averages and the 3-month bill discount yield; the New York Fed uses a bond-equivalent yield, so the sign differs in a few borderline months.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "How the Central Bank Pins the Overnight Rate",
  "ch5.law": "A central bank does not decree the interbank rate by fiat. It controls two levers: the quantity of reserves in the banking system, and the rates at which banks deposit or borrow reserves. The overnight rate settles where those two meet.",
  "ch5.p1": "Payments between banks ultimately settle using balances held at the central bank, called <strong>reserves</strong>. Accounts cannot end the day overdrawn: if a bank is short, it must borrow overnight from another bank or from the central bank at a penalty lending rate (the ceiling); if it has surplus cash, it earns the central bank's <strong>interest on reserves</strong> (the floor). On why reserves follow loans rather than the other way round, and why the textbook money multiplier fails, see <a href=\"../where-money-comes-from/#ch2\">Chapter 2 of Where Money Comes From</a>; here we focus only on how the price is set.",
  "ch5.p2": "In the morning, a bank does not know how much cash will flow in or out by evening. One extra dollar of reserves held overnight either sits idle (earning only the floor) or covers a late shortfall (saving the ceiling rate). What that marginal dollar is worth depends on the probability of running short. This is Poole's (1968) model:",
  "ch5.eq": "<i>i</i> = <i>i<sub>D</sub></i> + (<i>i<sub>L</sub></i> − <i>i<sub>D</sub></i>)·Φ((<i>K</i> − <i>R</i>)/<i>σ</i>)<br><span style=\"font-size:.85em;color:var(--mute)\"><i>R</i> reserves&emsp;<i>K</i> required end-of-day balance&emsp;<i>σ</i> intraday payment uncertainty&emsp;Φ standard normal CDF (shortfall probability)</span>",
  "ch5.model.title": "Reserve demand curve: corridor and floor",
  "ch5.model.how": "The cyan curve is bank demand for reserves: the scarcer reserves are, the higher the chance of an overdraft and the higher the rate banks will pay. The gold vertical line is reserve supply. Purple dots show 60 trading days as daily demand shifts randomly. Click 'Corridor' to watch the rate bounce between floor and ceiling; click 'Floor' to watch it hug the floor; drag the floor rate—the ceiling stays 2 pp above it (a width chosen for clarity).",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Reserve demand curve, central bank reserve supply, rate corridor bounds, and 60 daily overnight rates",
  "ch5.leg.demand": "Reserve demand",
  "ch5.leg.supply": "Reserve supply",
  "ch5.leg.bounds": "Floor (IORB) and ceiling (lending rate)",
